## Problem Statement

There are `n` servers numbered from `0` to `n - 1`.

Connections between servers are represented as:

```cpp
connections[i] = [ai, bi]

A connection is considered critical if removing it disconnects part of the network.

Return all critical connections in the network.

Intuition

A critical connection is also known as a Bridge in Graph Theory.

A bridge is an edge that, if removed, increases the number of connected components.

Instead of removing every edge and checking connectivity (which is expensive), we use Tarjan's Algorithm to find all bridges in a single DFS traversal.

Tarjan's Algorithm

For every node we maintain:

Discovery Time (dt)
dt[node]

The time when a node is first visited.

Low Time (low)
low[node]

The earliest discovered node reachable from the current node using:

Tree edges
Back edges
Bridge Condition

For an edge:

u ---- v

after DFS of v:

if(low[v] > dt[u])

then:

(u, v)

is a bridge.

Why?

Because v and its subtree cannot reach any ancestor of u using a back edge.

Therefore removing (u,v) disconnects the graph.

Algorithm
Build adjacency list.
Initialize:
discovery time array
low time array
Run DFS.
Update:
discovery times
low values
Whenever:
low[v] > dt[u]

store the edge as a bridge.

Return all bridges.
Dry Run

